    "raw": "First, let's determine the capacity of the second tank. Since the second tank is 450 liters and this represents 45% of its capacity, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[ 0.45 \\times \\text{Capacity} = 450 \\]\n\nSolving for the capacity:\n\n\\[ \\text{Capacity} = \\frac{450}{0.45} = 1000 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nSince both tanks have the same capacity, the first tank also has a capacity of 1000 liters.\n\nNext, we calculate how much more water is needed to fill each tank. For the first tank:\n\n\\[ 1000 - 300 = 700 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nFor the second tank:\n\n\\[ 1000 - 450 = 550 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nAdding these together gives the total amount of water needed:\n\n\\[ 700 + 550 = 1250 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nThus, the total amount of water needed to fill both tanks is 1250 liters.\n\nFINAL: 1250",
